[13:35:28] CALL write_file {'content': '# Tetris: An Explanation\n\nTetris is a classic tile-matching puzzle video game originally created in 1984 by Soviet software engineer Alexey Pajitnov. \n\n## Core Mechanics\n1. **Tetriminos**: Geometric shapes composed of four square blocks each, falling down from the top of the playing field (matrix). There are 7 standard shapes, commonly named after letters of the alphabet that they resemble: **I**, **J**, **L**, **O**, **S**, **T**, and **Z**.\n2. **Controls**: \n   - **Move Left / Right**: Shift the falling piece horizontally.\n   - **Soft Drop**: Accelerate the descent of the piece.\n   - **Hard Drop**: Instantly drop the piece to the bottom.\n   - **Rotate**: Turn the piece clockwise or counter-clockwise to fit gaps.\n3. **Line Clearing**: When a horizontal row of 10 blocks is completely filled with blocks and has no empty spaces, the row disappears, and all blocks above it shift down.\n4. **Scoring & Leveling**: Clearing multiple lines at once (especially four lines at once, known as a **"Tetris"**) awards more points. As the player clears more lines, the game level increases, causing pieces to fall faster.\n5. **Game Over**: The game ends when new tetriminos are prevented from entering the playfield because the stack has reached the top.\n', 'path': 'core/tetris.md'}
